[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '411,000 lines of code gone in one merge. The commit message called it a refactor. cal.com was the answer every time somebody asked for an open source Calendly, self-hosted, own your booking data, pay nobody. Bailey Pumfleet pushed'

[00:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the first commit in March 2021, and it grew into 48,000 stars and 437 contributors. Then, on April 15th, one pull request touched thousands of files,'

[00:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and nearly every line of it was a deletion. The repo was renamed to cal.diy, and the license went from AGPL to MIT, which sounds like a gift right up until'

[00:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you look at what\'s left in the box. Enjoying this so far? Subscribe to the YouTube channel and grab the free daily newsletter at indiehacker.news. New deep dive every weekday.'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Here\'s the readme, and the first thing on it is a warning. Use at your own risk, it says, "Strictly recommended for personal, non-production use." Right underneath sits a tip pointing you'

[01:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'somewhere else. For anything commercial or enterprise-ready, use cal.com, not this. Keep going, and the description is at least honest about itself. A fork with all the enterprise and commercial'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'code removed. Then, a short list of what walked out the door, and every item on it is something a company would need on day one. Down in the license section, they draw the line themselves. Unlike cal.com\'s open core model, this one has'

[01:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'nothing proprietary left in it. And at the very bottom, under contributing, is the sentence that actually matters. Contributions to this repo do not flow to cal.com\'s production platform. You'

[01:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can still send a pull request. It just doesn\'t go where it used to go. Worth remembering what this thing was'

[01:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'competing with. Calendly\'s free plan hands you one event type and one calendar connection, and the team tier runs $16 a seat every month. Cal.com\'s own hosted plans undercut that. $12 a'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'user for teams, 28 for organizations. Now go back to the directories that got deleted and line them up against those two tiers. They match and nobody cut those by accident. That is the price'

[02:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'list and their free repo stopped holding anything the paid plans charge for. It all happened inside one pull request. 4,085 files touched, 21,000 lines added, and'

[02:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '411,881 taken out. That works out at roughly 20 lines out for everyone that came in. To be fair about it, what\'s left is a'

[02:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'real product. You get unlimited event types and booking pages with no seat count and no license key attached. The app store still ships 113 apps. Calendar sync covers Google, Outlook, Apple, and'

[03:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'CalDAV, so your availability stays correct across every account you own. Video runs through Cal video, which is daily.co underneath, or Zoom and Google Meet if you\'d rather. You can take'

[03:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'payments through Stripe or PayPal, and either fire webhooks at whatever is listening, or drop the booker straight into your own site as an embed. For one person scheduling meetings on their own'

[03:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'domain, that is genuinely the entire job. Want to be featured? Pitch your open source repo at indiehacker.news/apply and get in front of thousands of'

[03:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'builders. 15 directories came out of that one folder. Teams went first, which takes shared availability with it, and any notion of a second person on your instance. Organizations followed, so no'

[03:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sub teams and no company sub domain. Round robin routing is gone, and that\'s the one that stinks because it\'s the reason most companies bother self-hosting a scheduler at all.'

[04:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Workflows went, too. So, there go the automated reminders that stop people no showing. Single sign-on and directory sync left the building along with impersonation, managed event types, and'

[04:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the admin deployment tooling. What you\'re holding now is a very good personal booking page. What you had in March was something a company could actually run. The stack underneath is about as'

[04:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mainstream as TypeScript gets. Next.js on the front, tRPC sitting between client and server so the calls stay typed end to end, React everywhere else. Data goes through Prisma into Postgres'

[04:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'version 13 or newer. Tailwind does the styling and the whole thing is a Turbo repo monorepo, which is how the web app and the doc site sit in the same tree as'

[04:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the API. You\'ll want Node 18 and Yarn, and there\'s one thing the diff doesn\'t tell you. The repo still clones at roughly 1.1 GB because a deletion commit never actually erases anything. Every'

[05:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'file they cut is still in there under the old license, one checkout away from anybody who wants it. Running it is the easy part. Clone the repo, run the install, then a single command spins up a local database and'

[05:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'seeds it with test users so you\'re not filling in a sign-up form before you can see anything actually work. It comes up on localhost 3000 and nothing about it phones home for permission or checks you'

[05:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'against the server somewhere. That part they did keep their word on. The months since are where it gets bleak. The last tagged release is version 6.2.0 dated the 1st of March, 6 weeks ahead of'

[05:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the rename, and nothing has shipped under the new name since. Container images are worse still. The cal.diy repository on Docker Hub holds zero images and zero pulls, while the old one'

[05:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sits at 4.8 million pulls and hasn\'t been pushed to since the 2nd of March. Somebody opened a pull request to point the publishing job back at the name space with the downloads. It was never merged, and the 10 people who wrote most'

[06:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of this code have gone quiet since the day of the rename. 618 commits in January, four in August. So, who is this actually for? Solo builders get a strong tool that cost'

[06:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'them nothing, and April didn\'t take that away. The trade you\'re making is stability because there\'s no version to pin and no image to pull. The deleted code never evaporated though. It\'s sitting in Git'

[06:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'history, so a serious community fork stays possible, and if enough small teams get burned, somebody will try it. Until then, take that warning literally. It was written by the company that owns'

[06:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the project, and businesses rarely trash a repo they still control. If self-hosting was anywhere near the plan for next quarter, better to find out now than in month three.'

[07:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And if you ever want to know where the money in a business like this really lives, check which folder they deleted.'
